I thought it was the Mikado too. But I then applied the usual rule of

   "if in doubt consult the manual"

The Shorter Oxford English Dictionary (not quite *the* manual to english, but
getting close) describes snickersnee by


  Snickersnee. v. and sb. Obs. or arch. 1613. Also "snick or snee",
    "snick and snee", "snick-a-snee". [alt of "snick or snee", repl. earlier
    "stick or snee" (XVII) - Du. "steken" thrust, STICK v., and "snee" dial. var.
    of "snijen", "snidjen" cut, repr. Gmc. "*snipan" (OE. "snipan" whence dial.
    "smithe").]
  [A] As vb. To thrust of cut in fighting with a knife; to use a knife in this
      manner, to fight with knives - 1802.
  [B] sb. 1. The practice of fighting or a combat with cut-and-thrust knives 1670
          2. transf. Used to denote one or other of two possible alternatives
             or courses - 1680
          3. A cut-and-thrust knife (in forms "snick-a-snee", "snickersnee") 1760

"Transcribing" this we can get the following information.

1) First known use of "snickersnee" is 1613 and is now consider obsolete or
   archaic.
2) it derives from "snick{ or | and |-a-}snee" which in turn derives from
   "stick or snee" which comes from a Dutch dialect version of "cut and thrust".
3) used as verb in early 1800s for knife fighting
4) earlier use as noun to describe cut and thrust fighting in 1670s and as
   a knife used in such fighting 1760.


I assume that W.S.Gilbert used the word for an executioners sword later because
of its sound rather than it describing the actual implement -- I don't expect
Japenese executioners decapitated people with 6" knives!

So perhaps snickersnee should be applied to daggers rather than katanas to
be linguistically accurate!

